THE MODULE-KEY AXIS PAYOFF. When one module path is a strict component-prefix
of the other, _module_matches's arm 2 decides whether the first extra
component names a TYPE inside the matched module (os/exec + Cmd -> match) or a
SIBLING module that shares the prefix (net/http + fcgi -> no match). It decided
that with extra[:1].isupper() -- Go's "capitals mean types", in a predicate
that takes no language and serves fifteen catalogues, and is constant-true
wherever module names are themselves capitalised (haskell 195/195 rows, swift
106/107, objc 126/131, elixir 250/529). Measured across eight repos in those
languages, arm 2 fired nine times and named a type ZERO times.

The rule is now _extra_component_names_a_type(parent, extra): a type is spelled
differently from the module it sits in, a sibling module the same way, so the
extra component decides by whether its case DISAGREES with the parent it
follows. Identical to the old test for a lowercase parent; rejects for a
capitalised one. One home, so scripts/measure-module-matches-arm2-ab.py can
patch exactly it.

The item's own analysis said no single route reaches removed=0 -- the
parent-agreement rule "fixes elixir completely and breaks haskell", because
Data.ByteString.Lazy.readFile really is a file read. So the benign firings
re-enter through the CATALOGUE, not the predicate: the bytestring siblings get
their own boot-library rows (arm 1, exact) and the Hackage extra/process-extras
packages get a new community overlay. Data.ByteString.Lazy and elixir's
Req.Response are orthographically identical and differ only semantically, which
no spelling rule can see -- the item's thesis, now shipped.

